;; Glasswood's thin ice with room to jump: twelve cells of it under five rows
;; of air. A jump from the bank lands a few cells onto the ice, and the far
;; bank is still more running than a second on the ice allows, so any way
;; over has the ice give under it (BRITTLE-TICKS). This is the fixture where
;; the crack itself does the work in the checker: in the one-tile corridors
;; (thin-ice-short, thin-ice-long) no walk lasts past WALK-MAX (60 ticks),
;; so nothing would stay on the ice long enough to crack, and a checker that
;; ignored the crack passed them all (sabotage S5).
(fixture
  name: "thin ice, twelve cells under open air: jump on and run, and it gives"
  expect: unreachable
  why: "BRITTLE-TICKS: a jump buys three or four cells, not twelve"
  start: (3 7)
  target: ((21 7))
  brittle: ((6 8) (7 8) (8 8) (9 8) (10 8) (11 8) (12 8) (13 8) (14 8) (15 8) (16 8) (17 8))
  grid:
  ("#########################"
   "#########################"
   "#########################"
   "#                       #"
   "#                       #"
   "#                       #"
   "#                       #"
   "#                       #"
   "#########################"
   "######~~~~~~~~~~~~#######"
   "#########################"))
